From 2233fe87e30eea0301f8cda9fc9a7f8526aeca57 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:25:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 265/280] WIP: media: rkvdec: allow up to 64 capture buffers

The capture queue does not set max_num_buffers, so videobuf2 limits it to
VB2_MAX_FRAME (32) buffers. Every reference frame must be a capture
buffer, so a userspace that keeps decoded frames queued for display or
further processing can exhaust that limit, and VIDIOC_CREATE_BUFS then
fails with -ENOBUFS.

Kodi's Dolby Vision profile 7 FEL playback holds up to 16 base layer
pictures while it waits for the matching enhancement layer pictures,
on top of the HEVC DPB and its render queue. The 4K base layer context
reached 32 capture buffers, decoding failed and playback showed a black
screen. Allow 64 capture buffers. They are still allocated on demand,
and the IOMMU-backed single-page allocations do not use CMA.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Christian Hewitt <christianshewitt@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c
index 4365304b5411..8c515262896b 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec.c
@@ -1479,6 +1479,7 @@ static int rkvdec_queue_init(void *priv,
 	dst_vq->timestamp_flags = V4L2_BUF_FLAG_TIMESTAMP_COPY;
 	dst_vq->lock = &rkvdec->vdev_lock;
 	dst_vq->dev = rkvdec->v4l2_dev.dev;
+	dst_vq->max_num_buffers = 64;
 
 	return vb2_queue_init(dst_vq);
 }
